  "live_records": {
    "q-167388": {
      "dom_id": "question-167388",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find the velocity of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $v(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m/s}$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14259041/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating $v(t)$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ we have\n\n$$\n3{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=3{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}=6.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
