    "q-167389": {
      "dom_id": "question-167389",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "${lim}_{t\\to {0}^{+}}v(t)\\,=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Substituting $t=0$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to {0}^{+}}v(t) & ={lim}_{t\\to {0}^{+}}3{csc}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =3{csc}^{2}⁡(0) \\\\  & =3\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14259041/assets/b01f56f0a78beb3b13799265975b1f4b16614e4a77d08aa24e024cf88309c4fc.png)\n\nAs $t$ approaches $0$ from the right, the values of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t$ are positive and grow without bound. \n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=\\infty \n$$\n\nApplying the product and power rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to {0}^{+}}3{csc}^{2}⁡(\\pi ) & ={lim}_{t\\to {0}^{+}}3\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to {0}^{+}}{csc}^{2}⁡(t) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {({lim}_{t\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(t))}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying by the positive constant $3$ does not change the sign of the limit, so we have\n\n$$\n3\\cdot {({lim}_{t\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(t))}^{2}=\\infty .\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
