    "q-167394": {
      "dom_id": "question-167394",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find the acceleration function ${a}_{2}(t)$ of the second particle.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${a}_{2}(t)\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "In part 3, we saw that the derivative of a particle's velocity with respect to time gives its acceleration.\n\nTo differentiate ${v}_{2}(t),$ we require the product rule. Let $g(t)=t$ and $h(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).$ Then\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(t)=1,\\,{h}^{′}(t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nWe used the chain rule to calculate ${h}^{′}(t).$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2}(t) & ={v}_{2}^{′}(t) \\\\  & ={g}^{′}(t)\\cdot h(t)+g(t)\\cdot {h}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(1)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))+(t)(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-4t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "dom_id": "",
          "tag": "mathquill",
          "choices": [],
          "dom_index": 0,
